WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Speaker 1: Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell&nbsp;
is not happy with the quality of candidates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.360
Republicans have been offering up for the 2022&nbsp;
midterms. And this story really feeds into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.840
growing rift within the Republican Party as&nbsp;
they try to figure out what direction they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.000
want to go in with Donald Trump's political&nbsp;
future being so uncertain at the moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.160
McConnell is speaking to reporters and he&nbsp;
sort of alluded to the fact that Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.000
really should be shoe ins, too, in both&nbsp;
the House and the Senate. If you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.880
historical precedent with we know the president&nbsp;
who's in power tending to have a bad first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.080
midterms and also the economic indicators that&nbsp;
aren't really going Democrats way. But McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.280
points out that some of these races tend to be&nbsp;
up to the personalities of the people running in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.760
them. And he mentions, without saying specifically&nbsp;
that a lot of these candidates are weak and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.800
wacky. So here's what Mitch McConnell had to say.
Speaker 4: I think there's probably a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.040
likelihood the House follows the Senate. Senate&nbsp;
races are just different. Their statewide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.880
candidate quality has a&nbsp;
lot to do with the outcome.&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.960
Speaker 1: So McConnell actually is quite right&nbsp;
on this, which is not something that I often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.360
say about him. But I do think that he's right on&nbsp;
both fronts here. Like, for one, Republicans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.280
definitely more favored to win the House than they&nbsp;
are the Senate. If you look at the FiveThirtyEight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.360
odds, Republicans currently have a 78% chance at&nbsp;
winning the lower chamber, but only a 37% chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.760
at flipping the upper chamber. And then yet people&nbsp;
generally want rational, competent, intelligent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.160
sound minded people representing them, regardless&nbsp;
of political affiliation. That's sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.040
the base level of things that people want in a&nbsp;
candidate and in a House race. Maybe you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.800
away with having a whack your candidate because&nbsp;
there's not going to be all that much attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.080
paid to them. But in a Senate race that's going to&nbsp;
get statewide attention, maybe even national media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.640
coverage. So you can always get away with having&nbsp;
a whack your candidate. And if you look around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.480
country, Republicans are not sending us their&nbsp;
best, to paraphrase Donald Trump. In Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.600
you have former football player Herschel Walker&nbsp;
who often fails to make any sense at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.320
And he hasn't really expressed that he knows&nbsp;
anything about policy matters. In Pennsylvania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.480
you have Mehmet Oz, who is coming off as an&nbsp;
elitist with $100 million and a carpetbagger from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.560
New Jersey who just thinks that he's entitled to&nbsp;
a Senate seat because he's been on television for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.320
so long. In Ohio, you have J.D. Vance, who went&nbsp;
from being a somewhat reasonable Republican who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.680
wrote the Hillbilly Elegy to now being a full on&nbsp;
Trumpist, who said that it's sometimes better for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.120
people in abusive relationships to stay together&nbsp;
rather than get divorced. And then in Wisconsin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.720
you have incumbent Ron Johnson, who often&nbsp;
spreads disinformation about the 2020 election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.880
voter fraud and COVID vaccines. And Wisconsin&nbsp;
is not a red state. It's a it's a blue state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.400
maybe a purple state, you could call it. So&nbsp;
they're definitely far to the left of where Ron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.640
Johnson is. So it's not surprising that he's also&nbsp;
losing in the polls. But at this point, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.480
no going back. Right. You're you're stuck with&nbsp;
most of these candidates. Most of the primaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.280
have already happened at this point. There's&nbsp;
about ten toss up races in the Senate this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.600
And I do think that Democrats are poised to&nbsp;
win much of them and hold on to their majority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.840
maybe even pick up a seat or two or three, which&nbsp;
would be awesome, because then we could talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.440
getting rid of the filibuster and pass&nbsp;
more of our progressive wish list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.120
assuming we're able to also hold on to the&nbsp;
House. But that's a conversation for another day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.360
But someone who did not like Mitch McConnell's&nbsp;
statements were Fox News propagandist Sean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.280
HANNITY. He said this about the statements.
Speaker 2: Now these same Democrats are painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.840
Republican Senate candidates running in upcoming&nbsp;
elections in midterms as cruel and out of touch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.800
Well, apparently, Senator Senate Minority Leader&nbsp;
